
CNet AMIGA will work on any model AMIGA computer.  A 1Meg system is
recommended for 1 or 2 ports.  Additional ports will require approximately
350K each.  Although it is POSSIBLE to run with 2 floppy drives, a hard drive 
IS recommended.

Here are some of the highlights of the software:

o Status line always contains user's handle and time of day, status window 
contains all user account information.  Status window can be toggled on and 
off.  Split screen mode allows 2 user's half screens to be displayed 
simulataneously.  Status window also contains current prompt and last command.
o Pull-down menus allow changing of these various screen display modes as well
(like chat mode, private BBS, etc).
o Full printer support, of "printer.device" set by preferences, for text 
output, may be turned on/off at any time.
o Support of 4800, 9600 baud, as well as 19.2 and higher "locked" baud rates, 
allowing for transfer throughput in excess of 1600 CPS. Drops DTR line to 
clear line. Also definable modem initialization sequences.
o Configuration for up to 15 modem ports, and one console port, multi-user 
system, completely written and protected for multi-user use. Will use any 
serial device/unit number combinations (ASDG, internal Supra Zi, etc, etc).
o Selection of screen depth at run time (8/4/2 colors) for speed, also 
"hidden" mode opens no screen at all, totally in the background.
o All BBS text contained externally, to save memory when running multiple 
ports, also to allow unlimited modification, like color addition, even formats
of Scan lists.  In addition, all Menus strings are contained externally to 
allow changing any command, aliasing several commands to one function, or 
adding new commands, which can be programmed to read files or run programs.
o Xmodem, CRC, 1K, Ymodem batch, and Zmodem fully supported, including Zmodem 
upload resume for unfinished files.
o Commodore C/G mode supported for 64/128 users to see color, cursor movement
o Extremely powerful text line editor supporting search/replace, bordering,
justification, paragraph boundaries, enhanced by an ANSI visual editor, which
supports full paragraph wrapping and auto-insert mode, mouse pointing, just
like any word processor.
o MCI (Message command interpreter) allows imbedding of commands whig(êprint 
variables, change colors, prompt for input, run programs, read other text 
files, test/branch, background colors -- access controllable.
o Up to 24 access "groups" each with a name, and a set of default 
ratios/limits (calls/day, minutes/call, file u/d ratios, byte u/d ratios, etc)
and default privelges ( more than 25 "flags" for system functions, and 
exemptions).
o Each user account (more than 2000 supported) contains complete address, as 
well as a copy of his access group's default ratios and priveleges, which may 
be customized for each user individually.  Other features here include 
TimeZone setting, AM/PM or military time preference, paranoid mode (won't let 
others see your address/birthdate), default protocol, and more.
o New user questionnaire optional for customized questions
o Function macro key support, 5 print-only, 5 input stream also.
o Full accounting system, supporting credits/debits for more than 15 system 
functions, with user balance, maximum debt, may also be used as an alternate 
"credit" system to credit for posts/responses, debit for downloading, etc.
o SAM (System Activity Monitor) tracks usage of the various system functions, 
and keeps "currents" so you know exactly how much of everything is on the 
system at any one time.  Also, SAG (System Acticity Graph) plots usage of the 
system to formulate a graph showinf peaks for busy times, etc.
o Time Restrictions, limited baud rates for Pfiles, Logon, UD, as well as 
access groups, for each of the 24 hours in a day.  Each port may be unique.
o Screen Dim function slowly lowers screen brightness is system is inactive.
o Each port may individually have it's own screen, selectable using the laying
gadgets and the mouse.  C-Net is very multitasking "friendly," if you have the
memory, run word processors, databases, other terminals programs in 
background.
o All maintenance/sysop function are performed on-line, including editing 
access groups, users (including those online, and yourself), also a real 
on-line CLI (password protected) to do just about ANYTHING on-line, including 
running programs using normal standard I/O. Edit/read any file, user 
incactivity purge, access "transpose" to reset default privs/ratios.
o Automaintenance mode, which can do user purge, old message/file deletion at 
any specified time during the day.
o Multiport systems can support inter-user conferencing, with "control" users 
to mediate conversation.  Also on-line messages between users.
o Electronic mail can be forwarded, and returnn-receipts can be requested. 
Also, sysops can edit user accounts directly.
o Call logs and u/d logs kept (optional verbosity levels)
o Auto-call back validation optionally calls users back to receive slightly 
higher access.
o All configuration of user files, access files done ON-LINE, nothing to do 
before running the program for the first time.
o Quick constant Alphabetical user organization routines for fast finding of 
speicific users, listing, etc.
o At logon, optional summary/search of all new messages on the system
o With subirectories, unlimited number of subboards possible.  Message/file 
areas may be u/d only, messages only, mixed, or static (no uploading/posting 
allowed).
o Direct exchange subboards allow users to directly list files on a disk or 
directory, select, download, upload, directly to the disk.
o Each subboard can contain an Entry text file message, and can be "joined" or
"dropped" by the each user.
o Up to 5 suboperators per subboard, with Mail to Subop option
o Subboards can be closed, restricted by age, password protected, access 
protected for entry, writing.  Also, optional "private message" areas, 
allowing users to leave Private posts, responses, even Files.
o Examine contents of Arc, Zoo, Zip, Lzh, and Grab contents of file (list in 
hex/ASCII format like CLI type opt h)
o Estimated download times, restricts downloading if no time
o Option to move items between subboards
o Option to alphabetize contents of subboard based on Titles.
o "Adopt orphins" directly from the disk into a subboard listing.
o Text search single item, all items, or globally.
o Validation supported, new uploads optionally may not be downloaded; uploads 
without descriptions are considered unvalidated. User receives no credit 
unless file is validated.  Users can remove unvalidated files.  Sysop has
option to remove credits when file is killed.
o Item attributes can be set, as a sysop favorite, no responses, free 
download, or protect from auto-deletion.
o Each item has a short description and a long description, responses to file 
descriptions are optional.  Short scan includes filesize and short description
o Option to allow users to change their Handles, addresses, etc themselves.
o Relogon option, use several accounts on one call.
o Built in BBSList system, Vote structured messages.  Although separate 
Gfiles, Pfiles, Vote, News sections, file types can be mixed (vote topics in 
News, Pfiles in Gfiles, etc).
o Download a Gfile with protocol
o AREXX language on-line program support, with full compatibility with all 
existing Skyline/Atredes files, executable from Pfiles, text files, MCI
o Program limits editor usage to maintain specific free disk space, uploads to
maintain a specified amount of disk space free.
o Full ANSI support if printed through AREXX files or text files.
o Set of menu commands "available everywhere" for ease of system use.
o Compiled and optimized C for fast, sure operation.

